o the Washington Post but so  vine says that while the numbers may be  small the personal impact is huge if you  are the  mother or the son or the brother or the  father of one of those individuals it is  still a priority in fact one of the  folks very much involved in our campaign  is one of those people who was arrested  at thrown in jail and still to this day  has ramifications within his life for  what he can and cannot do because of  psilocybin so vine also says she  believed Denver already had the  psychedelic culture necessary to be the  first to try decriminalization a culture  which is on display in the city's Rhyno  Art District that features buildings and  an entire alley covered with psychedelic  street art the psychedelics are already  here there's already a psychedelic  Renaissance happening we're seeing  different types of providers that are  coming in and now using this innate  pathways Maps up in Boulder who are  doing all these types of research  there's a lot more information available  here in this community and locally  around what's happening and they're  being used they're being used in the  music scene they're being used all over  we are looking at that and recognizing  that we don't think that people should  be going to jail or you know otherwise  is losing their livelihoods for the  result of using this substance Colorado  was one of the first two US states to  legalize recreational cannabis sales  which it did by voter initiative in 2012  putting it on the frontier of American  drug policy then and now this is the  best place in the country to do this  right now  Denver and maybe the state of Colorado  is in in many ways at least with drug  policy embracing more of this  libertarian concept that you know as  long as I'm not causing harm to self or  others we should be we should have the  freedom to use whatever substances we  choose and put into our bodies whatever  we choose Denver's decriminalization  initiative makes the possession or use  of psilocybin the city's lowest law  enforcement priority and prohibits the  city from spending resources to impose  criminal penalties for the personal use  and personal possession of psilocybin  mushrooms by persons 21 years of age and  older and also establishes a panel to  review the effects of the change in law  it does not legalize commercial sales of  mushrooms leaving a potentially large  gray market so vine says her experience  in cannabis legalization which  progressed from medical to recreational  every state that  legalized it convinced her that  decriminalization rather than  medicalization is actually the far more  radical and Liberatore path to take  maybe we want to see doctors being able  to prescribe psilocybin but is that the  only way we want to be able to access it  should we still be going to jail and  having it be a criminal offense outside  of having a physician prescribe it for  you lawfully I think that the core  tenant and the reason why the campaign  became what it was today was because we  don't necessarily think that going  through that process necessarily means  access to all patients groups like the  multidisciplinary Association for  psychedelic studies or Maps have spent  nearly 33 years funding the study of  psychedelics for treating mental and  emotional problems and that work is only  recently reaching fruition with a study  of the psychedelic MDMA to treat  post-traumatic stress disorder about to  enter the final phase of FDA clinical  trials and the organization is hopeful  it will achieve full approval for use in  clinical settings by 2021 scientists  like Charles Grove at Johns Hopkins have  also studied psilocybin in a clinical  setting and found a measurable decrease  in death anxiety among terminal cancer  patients dosed with the drug sometimes  just a single time our subjects reported  being more engaged in their lives  getting a renewed sense of self and a  renewed sense of meaning and purpose to  the time they had remaining but  advocates say the more radical  decriminalization movement complements  rather than undermines the work of  groups like maps psilocybin mushrooms  have the potential to address the five  psychiatric symptoms that we're facing  as a country and those things are  depression anxiety addiction PTSD and  pain well the research is amazing it it  costs a lot of money and takes a lot of  time and the fact that people are being  criminalized today as we speak is a big  motivation for why we're doing this work  on the horizon activists are working to  put statewide psilocybin  decriminalization on ballots in Oregon  in California in 2020 both so vine and  Matthews believe that what happened in  Denver won't stay in Denver  part of a team from California is here  tonight now they came out to support us  and I know that petitioners in Oregon  the the best of mice I can offer is to  find volunteers who are committed to  this fundraising is key really respect  and honor the research and the  researchers who have dedicated their  entire lives to this work they need to  be a part of the conversation it starts  by forcing the the conversation and and  protecting access whether you're using  this plant for a medical reason or a  spiritual reason or a recreational  reason you should not be going to jail  or losing your children for it  [Music]
 

